          "question": "I have been anxious for months and it is starting to interfere with daily life. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line treatment, and where does it sit in ICD-10-CM classification?",
          "answer": "If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice (lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity, lifestyle-hypertension-activity, condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder, guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy, condition-insomnia). The risk assessment indicates a \"routine\" urgency, meaning there's no immediate emergency pattern detected from your description, but this assessment is based on a fixed list of red-flag presentations, and a symptom not on this list can still be serious (lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity, lifestyle-hypertension-activity, condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder, guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy, condition-insomnia). If you are experiencing anxiety that is interfering with your daily life, it is important to discuss this with a clinician, who can help with an accurate diagnosis and develop a suitable treatment plan (guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care, condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder, lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity, guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management, guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line, coding-icd10-cm-versus-who-icd10, guideline-osteoarthritis-management, condition-insomnia, coding-icd10-chapter-05-mental, coding-insomnia-g47, coding-icd10-chapter-04-endocrine).\n\nAnxiety is assessed using screening instruments like the seven-item generalized anxiety disorder questionnaire, where a score of 10 is often the threshold for further assessment (guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care).\n\nFirst-line treatments for generalized anxiety disorder can include structured exercise, relaxation, mindfulness practice, and guided digital programs (condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder). Moderate aerobic activity, such as brisk walking, cycling, or swimming, is described as a useful adjunct to other treatments (lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity). Benzodiazepines are not considered first-line treatment due to concerns about tolerance, dependence, cognitive effects, falls, and their lack of effect on the underlying disorder; if used, their role is short and limited (guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care).\n\nAnxiety, dissociative, stress-related, somatoform, and other non-psychotic mental disorders are classified in the ICD-10-CM system under codes F40 to F48 (ICD-10-CM Chapter 5: mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental disorders (F01-F99)). This range includes conditions like phobias, panic disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder, and reactions to severe stress, including post-traumatic stress disorder (ICD-10-CM Chapter 5: mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental disorders (F01-F99)). ICD-10-CM is the United States clinical modification of the World Health Organization's ICD-10, and it subdivides the categories much further than the international version (coding-icd10-cm-versus-who-icd10).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
